---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/services.css';
import '../../styles/editorial-pages.css';
const rows = [
  ['Core collaboration',120,120,120,'Essential shared work; retain'],
  ['Accounting',60,60,60,'Essential records; retain'],
  ['CRM',80,100,120,'Investigate the plan or seat increase'],
  ['AI assistant seats',60,60,60,'One of two $30 seats assumed inactive'],
  ['Meeting recorder',30,30,30,'Assumed unused; confirm retention needs'],
  ['Second design tool',50,50,50,'Assumed overlap; check files and features'],
];
const totals = [1,2,3].map(i => rows.reduce((sum,row) => sum + Number(row[i]),0));
const money = (value: number) => new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US',{style:'currency',currency:'USD',maximumFractionDigits:0}).format(value);
---
<BaseLayout title="Example Reviews | Cloud & Capital" description="Fictional demonstrations of a Technology Cost Review and an AI & Workflow Review, with labeled assumptions, calculations, and next steps.">
  <main id="main-content" class="offer-page example-page"><div class="offer-shell">
    <p class="offer-eyebrow">What the work can look like</p><h1>Two questions.<br /><em>Two useful reviews.</em></h1>
    <div class="example-note"><p><strong>Fictional examples.</strong> The businesses, records, costs, timings, and outcomes below are invented to demonstrate the proposed services. These are not client results or testimonials.</p></div>
    <div class="offer-actions"><a href="#cost">Technology costs</a><a href="#workflow">Recurring reporting</a><a href="/services">Back to services →</a></div>
    <section id="cost" class="example-section" aria-labelledby="cost-title"><p class="offer-eyebrow">Technology Cost Review</p><h2 id="cost-title">“Our tools keep getting more expensive.<br />What should we keep or change?”</h2><p>A fictional agency supplies three billing summaries, subscription details, and illustrative usage and renewal information. No annual prepaid charges or taxes are included.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Fictional subscription costs"><table><caption>Illustrative monthly bills in USD</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Subscription</th><th scope="col">Month 1</th><th scope="col">Month 2</th><th scope="col">Month 3</th><th scope="col">Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows.map(row => <tr><th scope="row">{row[0]}</th><td>{money(Number(row[1]))}</td><td>{money(Number(row[2]))}</td><td>{money(Number(row[3]))}</td><td>{row[4]}</td></tr>)}</tbody><tfoot><tr><th scope="row">Total</th>{totals.map(total => <td><strong>{money(total)}</strong></td>)}<td>Three months: {money(totals.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0))}</td></tr></tfoot></table></div>
      <h3>What the numbers support</h3><p>Latest spend is {money(totals[2])}/month, equivalent to {money(totals[2]*12)}/year if unchanged. That is a run-rate estimate, not a historical annual bill. The CRM increase explains the entire $40 rise between Months 1 and 3; its cause needs checking.</p>
      <h3>Three conditional opportunities</h3><p>Removing an inactive AI seat ($30), an unused recorder ($30), and an overlapping design tool ($50) would reduce spend by $110/month, or $1,320 over twelve full months after the changes take effect. An assumed $120 file-cleanup cost reduces that benefit to $1,200 before any review fee.</p><p>Confirm actual use, dependencies, notice terms, and export feasibility first. A delayed cancellation reduces first-year savings; a missing feature may make keeping the tool worthwhile.</p>
      <h3>Action order</h3><ol><li>Owner checks the AI seat’s user and dependencies before removing it.</li><li>Team checks recorder use and record-retention needs.</li><li>Preserve required design files and verify remaining features before changing tools.</li><li>Ask the CRM vendor for plan and seat-change history; retain the system pending that answer.</li><li>Compare the next actual bill with the intended changes.</li></ol><p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> retain core systems, check three specific opportunities, investigate the CRM increase. Account changes and migrations are outside the review.</p><p class="scope-note">Missing information is labeled. Annual charges need separate reconciliation; duplicate transaction records need explicit resolution rather than double-counting.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="workflow" class="example-section" aria-labelledby="workflow-title"><p class="offer-eyebrow">AI &amp; Workflow Review</p><h2 id="workflow-title">“Can AI stop us copying the same numbers<br />into every report?”</h2><p>A fictional consultancy produces eight monthly project reports using a time tracker, accounting exports, and a spreadsheet. All timings below are assumed.</p><h3>Follow the work</h3><p>Export time and invoices → align project names → copy rows → calculate labor and expenses → check totals and missing entries → prepare a summary.</p><p>The bottleneck hypothesis is inconsistent project identifiers and repeated copying. AI-written commentary would not fix those inputs and could add checking work.</p>
      <h3>Compare options</h3><ul><li><strong>Controlled reporting template:</strong> first pilot; standardize identifiers and calculations.</li><li><strong>Existing native features:</strong> check actual plans and export capabilities before buying anything.</li><li><strong>Small automation:</strong> consider after inputs are consistent, with an owner for failures and maintenance.</li><li><strong>AI commentary:</strong> defer until input sensitivity, accuracy checks, and human judgment are addressed.</li></ul>
      <h3>One pilot, with checks</h3><p>Use two completed projects and redacted exports. Reconcile supplied totals, flag missing hours, flag duplicates, stop unmatched identifiers disappearing, and confirm that an added expense updates its report. A named staff member owns updates and final review.</p>
      <h3>Capacity is different from cash saving</h3><p>Eight reports × 90 minutes = 12 hours/month. A target of eight × 45 minutes = six hours, plus one hour of maintenance, would free five net hours/month. At an assumed $40/hour, that is $200/month of capacity value.</p><p><strong>That is not $200 of cash saving.</strong> A financial saving requires an actual expense reduction; revenue benefit needs evidence of productive use. The target has not been measured.</p><p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> stabilize inputs and reporting before adding AI. Compare actual preparation, checking, maintenance, exceptions, and usability across two report cycles. Implementation is separately scoped.</p>
    </section><div class="offer-actions"><a class="offer-button" href="/services#inquiry">Tell me about your question →</a></div>
  </div></main>
</BaseLayout>
